### Datasets

- [HarmBench](https://huggingface.co/datasets/walledai/HarmBench), released by Walled AI and the HarmBench authors under the MIT license. Please cite [Mazeika et al., 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04249).
- [AdvBench](https://huggingface.co/datasets/walledai/AdvBench), distributed under the MIT license. This repository downloads its `harmful_behaviors.csv` from the [LLM Attacks repository](https://github.com/llm-attacks/llm-attacks/blob/main/data/advbench/harmful_behaviors.csv). Please cite [Zou et al., 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15043).
- [UltraChat 200k](https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceH4/ultrachat_200k), curated by Hugging Face H4. The dataset card requests citation of [Ding et al., 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14233).

The model and dataset licenses are separate from this repository's license and
may include additional access or attribution requirements.

## Setup

Create a Python environment and install dependencies:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Running the pipeline

### 1) Download datasets

```bash
python download_datasets.py
```

### 2) Extract latent vectors

```bash
python extract_vectors.py
```

This script loads the model, extracts one hidden-state vector per prompt for the selected layer, and saves them to the data directory.

### 3) Run the anomaly detector

```bash
python multi_vector_repe.py
```

This script:

- loads cached latent vectors
- splits data into calibration and test sets
- clusters malicious calibration samples into K malicious sub-manifolds
- evaluates the detector on held-out prompts
- prints ROC-AUC and attack detection statistics
- exports misclassified prompts (false alarms and missed attacks) to a timestamped CSV in `data/<model_id>_multi_vector_repe_results_<timestamp>.csv` for error analysis

## Expected outputs

The detector reports summary metrics to the console:

- ROC-AUC score
- decision threshold based on the benign distribution (e.g., 99th percentile)
- benign pass rate
- false alarms (`False Positive`)
- attacks detected (`True Positive`)
- attacks missed (`False Negative`)

The current implementation uses a calibration set of 100 benign and 100 malicious samples, with the remainder reserved for evaluation.

### Error analysis CSV export

At the end of evaluation, `multi_vector_repe.py`, `early_exit_detection.py`, and baseline detectors automatically filter and export misclassified prompts to a CSV file in `data/`, prefixed by the active model ID (e.g. `data/<model_id>_<script_results>_<timestamp>.csv`):

- **Only misclassifications are saved**: filters specifically for `False Alarm (False Positive)` (benign prompts incorrectly flagged as malicious) and `Missed Attack (False Negative)` (malicious prompts that evaded detection). Correctly classified prompts are omitted to keep error triage focused.
- **Sorted by score**: rows are sorted descending by detector score (`RepE_Harm_Score` or anomaly score) so the highest-confidence misclassifications appear first.
- **Columns included**: `Ground_Truth`, `Test_Set`, `RepE_Harm_Score`, `Prediction`, `Prompt_Text`, and `Result_Status`.

### 4) Optional: extract all-layer latent states

```bash
python extract_all_layers.py
```

This script performs the same model pass but keeps the normalized hidden state from every transformer layer, saving a 3D array of shape `(num_prompts, num_layers, hidden_dim)` for both benign and malicious prompts to `data/<model_id>_all_layers.npz`.

This is useful when you want to inspect which layer is most informative before committing to a single chosen layer for the full detector.

### 4a) Optional: analyze layer-by-layer detection quality

```bash
python analyze_all_layers.py
```

This script loads the all-layer cache, computes a simple RepE score at each layer, and plots ROC-AUC as a function of layer depth. It saves a chart to `data/<model_id>_layer_trajectory.png` and helps identify the strongest hidden layer for downstream analysis.

### 4b) Optional: run early-exit detection tests

```bash
python early_exit_detection.py
```

This script calibrates a harmful-direction threshold from the selected layer, then evaluates the early-exit detector across benign prompts (UltraChat), base malicious attacks (HarmBench), and adversarial prompt variants such as GCG-style suffix attacks and roleplay/developer-mode jailbreak wrappers. It is useful for checking whether a discovered layer remains robust under stronger attack patterns beyond the normal evaluation split.

Like the main detector, it automatically exports misclassified prompts (false alarms and missed attacks across base attacks, GCG suffixes, and roleplay wrappers) to `data/<model_id>_early_exit_detection_results_<timestamp>.csv`.


## Model configuration

Models and their designated evaluation layers (`layer_idx` and `auc_layer`) are managed via `config/config.yml`. Preconfigured models include:

- `google/gemma-2-2b` (`layer_idx: 14`, `auc_layer: 20`)
- `meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct` (`layer_idx: 14`, `auc_layer: 12`)
- `Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct` (`layer_idx: 18`, `auc_layer: 21`)

To switch the active model, set `model.name` under `model:` in `config/config.yml`.

## Notes

- This is a research-focused pipeline for model safety and anomaly detection.
- Inference can be computationally expensive, so a GPU is recommended.
- The layer index used for vector extraction is configurable in `config/config.yml`.
- The project is intended for experimentation and analysis of malicious latent structure rather than deployment in production systems.

## Related ideas

This work is inspired by the broader literature on:

- representation engineering (RepE)
- latent-space anomaly detection
- activation steering and safety probing
- multi-manifold modeling of harmful behavior

